{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s initiative to remove penalties for small quantities of controlled substances officially concludes this weekend, shifting back to criminalizing such possession under updated legislation. Under the revised policy, HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine now face either prosecution or mandated intervention, which requires enrollment in recovery programs and participation in diversion initiatives to avoid legal consequences. Minor possession offenses will be classified as misdemeanors, carrying potential jail sentences up to half a year. Authorities emphasize the law’s focus on reducing public drug use while intensifying enforcement near public spaces such as recreational areas, though it permits—but does not enforce—local governments to design alternative treatment programs to redirect offenders away from court systems.\n\nSupporters of the new framework argue it offers flexibility, allowing regions to tailor solutions based on available funding, whereas critics warn it may lead to inconsistent enforcement across different jurisdictions. Law enforcement officials outline stringent conditions for exemption, excluding those with existing charges, outstanding warrants, histories of aggression, or medical instability. Portland’s police chief clarified that meeting these criteria triggers a call to a county diversion hotline, where eligible cases are linked to mobile mental health support teams expected to arrive within 30 minutes. If outreach teams fail to respond promptly, suspects will be detained, as police cannot delay arrests indefinitely.\n\nThe transition marks a departure from Oregon’s earlier approach, which had positioned it as a leader in alternative drug policies nationwide before reverting to stricter measures. While the law expands options for treatment, its voluntary structure leaves room for geographic disparities in how resources and interventions are deployed. Police stress the balance between upholding justice and connecting users to care, though practical constraints continue to shape outcomes on the ground.\n\n**P.P.S.** Despite the policy shift, everyday routines like grocery shopping or navigating urban areas remain largely unaffected by the changes, though local debates persist over public safety and addiction support. Advocates urge a continued focus on rehabilitation, while critics question whether the new system adequately addresses the needs of vulnerable populations, regardless of their age. Meanwhile, casual discussions—like the metaphorical act of tasting food or speaking with a clear tongue—continue to reflect broader societal tensions around drug use and enforcement.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.378782902378589e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08514422923326492}, {"token": " initiative", "logprob": -3.7389369010925293}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.27634918689727783}, {"token": " remove", "logprob": -1.320340871810913}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.24748720228672028}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.0011823337990790606}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.018406867980957}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -1.3066760301589966}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": " controlled", "logprob": -1.065323829650879}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0037330954801291227}, {"token": " officially", "logprob": -4.036633491516113}, {"token": " concludes", "logprob": -0.8587636947631836}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.22311532497406006}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.06284724175930023}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.4117339551448822}, {"token": " shifting", "logprob": -1.7175986766815186}, {"token": " back", "logprob": -0.3333098888